{title: Molly Malone (Cockles and Mussels)}
{artist: James Yorkston, 1883}
{key: C}
{tempo: 120}
{time: 3/4}
{difficulty: beginner}
{tags: folk, irish, waltz}

{start_of_verse: Verse 1}
In [C]Dublin's fair [Am]city, where the [Dm]girls are so [G7]pretty
I [C]first set my [Am]eyes on sweet [Dm]Molly Ma[G7]lone
As she [C]wheeled her wheel[Am]barrow through [Dm]streets broad and [G7]narrow
Crying [C]"Cockles and [Am]mussels, a[G7]live, alive [C]oh!"

{start_of_chorus}
A[C]live, alive [Am]oh! A[Dm]live, alive [G7]oh!
Crying [C]"Cockles and [Am]mussels, a[G7]live, alive [C]oh!"

{start_of_verse: Verse 2}
She [C]was a fish[Am]monger, and [Dm]sure 'twas no [G7]wonder
For [C]so were her [Am]father and [Dm]mother be[G7]fore
And they [C]each wheeled their [Am]barrow through [Dm]streets broad and [G7]narrow
Crying [C]"Cockles and [Am]mussels, a[G7]live, alive [C]oh!"

{start_of_verse: Verse 3}
She [C]died of a [Am]fever, and [Dm]no one could [G7]save her
And [C]that was the [Am]end of sweet [Dm]Molly Ma[G7]lone
Now her [C]ghost wheels her [Am]barrow through [Dm]streets broad and [G7]narrow
Crying [C]"Cockles and [Am]mussels, a[G7]live, alive [C]oh!"
